WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.124
A practical guide to subtitles: SRT, VTT and ASS.

2
00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:10.994
The three subtitle formats you will meet, and how to fix the usual problems:

3
00:00:10.994 --> 00:00:14.731
wrong format, broken letters, drifting timing and missing tracks.

4
00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:20.613
The three main formats. SubRip is the most common subtitle format there

5
00:00:20.613 --> 00:00:20.803
is.

6
00:00:22.103 --> 00:00:24.820
Problem 1: the player will not load the file.

7
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.391
Usually a format mismatch.

8
00:00:27.691 --> 00:00:29.870
Problem 4: no subtitles at all.

9
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:35.143
If the subtitles are inside the video file, as they often are in MKV files,

10
00:00:35.143 --> 00:00:40.064
Extract Subtitles pulls the text tracks out as SRT, VTT, ASS and more.

11
00:00:41.364 --> 00:00:43.483
My usual flow for a talk recording.

12
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.904
All four steps run in the browser, so an unreleased recording stays on my

13
00:00:47.904 --> 00:00:49.236
laptop the whole time.

14
00:00:50.536 --> 00:00:53.473
To try it, open Subtitle Converter in OpenFile.

15
00:00:53.473 --> 00:00:57.035
Drop in a file, adjust the settings and press the button.

16
00:00:57.035 --> 00:01:00.221
It all runs on your device, so nothing is uploaded.

17
00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:07.113
Read the full post, with live demos, on the OpenFile blog.
